  "live_records": {
    "q-280961": {
      "dom_id": "step-q280961",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "The remainder of dividing $2{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x-7$ by $x+1$ is{{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Let $p(x)=2{x}^{4}+{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}-3x-7.$ \n\nThe remainder theorem states that when $p(x)$ is divided by $(x+1),$ the remainder equals $p(-1).$ \n\nEvaluating $p(-1)$ directly gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(-1) & =2(-1)^{4}+(-1)^{3}-2(-1)^{2}-3(-1)-7 \\\\  & =2-1-2+3-7 \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, when $p(x)$ is divided by $(x+1),$ the remainder is $-5.$\n\n**Note:** We can check the answer by carrying out synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $-1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-3$ | $-7$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-2$ | $1$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n|  | $2$ | $-1$ | $-1$ | $-2$ | $-5$ |",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "dom_id": "freeResponseTextbox-1",
