    "q-112672": {
      "dom_id": "step-q112672",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find the remainder of the division $\\frac{-3{x}^{4}+7{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-x+7}{x-2}.$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $p(x)=-3{x}^{4}+7{x}^{3}-4{x}^{2}-x+7.$ \n\nThe remainder theorem states that when $p(x)$ is divided by $(x-2),$ the remainder equals $p(2).$ \n\nEvaluating $p(2)$ directly gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(2) & =-3(2)^{4}+7(2)^{3}-4(2)^{2}-2+7 \\\\  & =-48+56-16-2+7 \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, when $p(x)$ is divided by $(x-2),$ the remainder is $-3.$\n\n**Note:** We can check the answer by carrying out synthetic division:\n\n|  | ${x}^{4}$ | ${x}^{3}$ | ${x}^{2}$ | ${x}^{1}$ | ${x}^{0}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $2$ | $-3$ | $7$ | $-4$ | $-1$ | $7$ |\n|  | $↓$ | $-6$ | $2$ | $-4$ | $-10$ |\n|  | $-3$ | $1$ | $-2$ | $-5$ | $-3$ |",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
