    "q-271059": {
      "dom_id": "step-q271059",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "When ${x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}+x+a$ is divided by $x+7$ the remainder is $2.$ What is the value of $a?$\n\n$a=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "The remainder theorem states that when $q(x)$ is divided by $(x+7),$ the remainder equals $q(-7).$ Since we're told that the remainder is $2,$ then we must have $q(-7)=2.$\n\nEvaluating $q(-7)$ directly gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q(-7) & =(-7)^{3}+7(-7)^{2}+(-7)+a \\\\  & =-343+343-7+a \\\\  & =-7+a,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n2=-7+a.\n$$\n\nSolving for $a$ in the equation above, we find that $a=9.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
