    "q-271169": {
      "dom_id": "step-q271169",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "If the remainder of the division $\\frac{{x}^{4}-p{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+5x+7}{x+2}$ is $q,$ express $p$ in terms of $q.$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "Let $f(x)={x}^{4}-p{x}^{3}+2{x}^{2}+5x+7.$ \n\nThe remainder theorem states that when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+2),$ the remainder equals $f(-2).$ Since we're told that the remainder is $q,$ then, we must have $f(-2)=q.$\n\nEvaluating $f(-2)$ directly gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-2) & =(-2)^{4}-p(-2)^{3}+2(-2)^{2}+5(-2)+7 \\\\  & =16+8p+8-10+7 \\\\  & =21+8p,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\nq=21+8p.\n$$\n\nSolving for $p$ in the equation above, we find that $p=\\frac{q-21}{8}.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "dom_id": "freeResponseTextbox-1",
          "tag": "mathquill",
          "choices": [],
