    "q-112682": {
      "dom_id": "step-q112682",
      "name": "Question 6",
      "problem": "When $p(x)=9{x}^{3}+m{x}^{2}+x-2$ is divided by $3x-1$ the remainder is $2.$ What is the value of $m?$",
      "worked_solution": "The remainder theorem states that when $p(x)$ is divided by $3x-1=3(x-\\frac{1}{3}),$ the remainder equals $p(\\frac{1}{3}).$ Since we're told that the remainder is $2,$ then, we must have $p(\\frac{1}{3})=2.$\n\nEvaluating $p(\\frac{1}{3})$ directly gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(\\frac{1}{3}) & =9{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{3}+m{(\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}+\\frac{1}{3}-2 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{m}{9}+\\frac{1}{3}-2 \\\\  & =\\frac{m}{9}-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{m-12}{9},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\n2=\\frac{m-12}{9}.\n$$\n\nSolving for $m$ in the equation above, we find that $m=30.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
