    "q-18146": {
      "dom_id": "step-q18146",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Suppose $f(x)=a{x}^{2}+bx-6$ has a factor of $(x-3)$ and has a remainder of $-4$ when divided by $(x+1).$ Find the value of $a+b.$",
      "worked_solution": "Since $(x-3)$ is a factor of $f(x)$, it must divide into $f(x)$ with no remainder. In other words, when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x-3),$ the remainder is $0.$ It follows from the remainder theorem that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0 & =f(3) \\\\  & =a(3)^{2}+b(3)-6 \\\\  & =a(9)+b(3)-6 \\\\  & =9a+3b-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLikewise, since we're told that the remainder is $-4$ when $f(x)$ is divided by $(x+1),$ it follows from the remainder theorem that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4 & =f(-1) \\\\  & =a(-1)^{2}+b(-1)-6 \\\\  & =a(1)+b(-1)-6 \\\\  & =a-b-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have the system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}0=9a+3b-6 \\\\ -4=a-b-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimplifying each equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3a+b=2 \\\\ a-b=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo eliminate $b,$ we add the equations and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3 & a+b &  & =2 \\\\ +\\, & a-b &  & =2 \\\\ 4 & a &  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get $a=1.$ Finally, we substitute it into $a-b=2$ to find $b=-1.$ \n\nTherefore, our final solution is $(a,b)=(1,-1).$ \n\nFinally then, $a+b=1-1=0.$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-18146-a",
